[[{:db/id 17592186261938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eab4249-544e-56c6-81c0-e6db84033701" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance Given the Number, Sum, and Sum of Squares of the Data Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79aa2e40-f4d2-5709-8b5b-54790b86acf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186261940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79aa2e40-f4d2-5709-8b5b-54790b86acf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186261940} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5dbac9f-85f4-59c4-8a53-10bde3633c1d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186261939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7dfa7943-f9fd-587a-96e7-82a3ae160d16" :question/math-academy-id "q-87562" :question/problem "Compute the variance ${\\sigma }^{2}$ of a data set ${x}_{i}$ consisting of $9$ data points, where\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=27,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2}=117.\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=9$ points in our data set, and we know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=27,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2}=117,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 117 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =13-{(3)}^{2} \\\\  & =13-9 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]]
